[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce any “session keeps resetting” behavior on the network. The control-plane between the border devices (router1 and router2 running FRR/BGP) is stable: BGP is Established on both ends, with 0 drops and an Up/Down timer incrementing normally. Routes are correctly exchanged (router1 learns 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2; router2 learns 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1). Data-plane tests from pc1 to pc2 show 0% packet loss both for standard pings and DF 1500-byte probes, indicating no MTU/PMTUD issues. Interfaces on both routers (eth0/eth1) are UP with no errors/drops, tc stats show no queue drops, and ethtool reports stable links.

Mechanism check: if BGP were flapping, we would see neighbor drops and route withdrawals, which would cause transient loss. Instead, FRR logs show only a single connection reset coincident with daemon start (“Connection reset by peer” at 17:50:37), after which keepalives and updates proceed normally with no subsequent resets.

Localization: All evidence points to healthy operation on router1:eth0/eth1 and router2:eth0/eth1, and correct routing on both PCs.

Root cause: No active network fault; the only observed reset was a one-time BGP re-establishment at FRR startup.

Corrective fix: None required on the network at this time. If users still experience application “hang ups,” investigate endpoints (pc1/pc2) or the application itself. For added assurance, enable BGP flap dampening/monitoring and consider BFD if very fast failure detection is desired, but current stability indicates the interconnect is sound. 
=== END ===